WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:01.760
COLT MCANLIS: Google
Cloud Next 2017

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:03.920
hosted more talks detailing
the future of cloud

00:00:03.920 --> 00:00:05.510
than you can shake a stick at.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:08.480
If you didn't get a chance to
catch Yufeng Guo's session,

00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:10.960
On-Device Machine Learning,
TensorFlow on Android,

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.710
then stay tuned, because
here's the recap.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.190
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.890
By combining TensorFlow with
your mobile applications,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.760
you can create magical AI
experiences for your users

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:24.469
based on their custom data sets.

00:00:24.469 --> 00:00:26.510
RETO MEIER: This is tricky,
though, since today's

00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:28.430
mobile devices don't
have enough power

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:31.610
to train AI models, not to
mention that it takes terabytes

00:00:31.610 --> 00:00:34.375
of high-quality training data to
build a new model from scratch.

00:00:34.375 --> 00:00:35.750
COLT MCANLIS: The
solution, then,

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:37.880
is to split the
difference, leveraging

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:40.760
the power of the cloud to
drive your mobile experience.

00:00:40.760 --> 00:00:43.940
Creating new machine learning
models is extremely resource

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:46.100
intensive, so the most
practical approach

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:50.210
is to use cloud ML to train your
TensorFlow data in the cloud,

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:53.720
and then deploying your trained
model on your iOS, Android,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.382
Raspberry Pi devices.

00:00:55.382 --> 00:00:56.840
RETO MEIER: A great
example of this

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.400
is adding customized image
recognition to your mobile app

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.534
that works in real
time and offline.

00:01:03.534 --> 00:01:05.450
We start by gathering
the training data, which

00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:08.000
includes the objects we
want to recognize in lots

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:10.880
of variations-- multiple
angles, different lighting,

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:13.070
different backgrounds
and orientations.

00:01:13.070 --> 00:01:16.580
The images of each object get
stored in its own Google Cloud

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.142
Storage folder, ready for
use in training the model.

00:01:19.142 --> 00:01:21.350
COLT MCANLIS: Training a
brand new model from scratch

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:23.700
is a time- and
resource-intensive process,

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:24.860
so we're going to cheat.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.290
By using cloud ML, we
can leverage a technique

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.690
known as transfer
learning, which

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.980
lets us alter the final layer of
Google's massive 48-layer image

00:01:33.980 --> 00:01:36.410
recognition model, called
Inception v3, which

00:01:36.410 --> 00:01:39.560
handles categorization and
recognition of objects.

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.510
By leveraging the
existing model,

00:01:41.510 --> 00:01:45.277
we can train on fewer
examples and in far less time.

00:01:45.277 --> 00:01:47.360
RETO MEIER: Now, once your
model has been trained,

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.780
you need to optimize it before
incorporating it into your app.

00:01:50.780 --> 00:01:53.240
A newly trained model is
typically around 80 megs,

00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:56.600
so we want to trim that down
before putting it on a device.

00:01:56.600 --> 00:01:58.190
To start with, we
can remove parts

00:01:58.190 --> 00:02:01.250
of the model which are used only
for training, which can't be

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:03.034
used on those devices anyway.

00:02:03.034 --> 00:02:04.700
COLT MCANLIS: We can
shrink it even more

00:02:04.700 --> 00:02:07.010
using a technique
called quantization.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.500
Here, we take the 32-bit
floating point values

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.480
used to represent
each pixel in an image

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:13.760
and reduce it to 8-bit values.

00:02:13.760 --> 00:02:15.440
While this is lossy,
the neural network

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:17.960
is designed to take fuzzy
input into account when

00:02:17.960 --> 00:02:21.140
doing identifications, so we can
lose significant image quality

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:23.480
without sacrificing accuracy.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:26.780
Using this approach alone, we
can reduce our model by 4x,

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.622
bringing it down
to about 20 megs.

00:02:28.622 --> 00:02:30.830
RETO MEIER: Now, when our
optimizations are complete,

00:02:30.830 --> 00:02:32.260
we can deploy our model.

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:35.060
Now, keep in mind that the
model itself is still 20 megs,

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:37.880
so while you can package it
directly within your app,

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:39.320
you may want to
make it available

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:40.314
as a separate download.

00:02:40.314 --> 00:02:42.230
COLT MCANLIS: Check out
the full session video

00:02:42.230 --> 00:02:45.680
to see Yufeng demonstrate his
amazing candy sorter demo,

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.180
and learn more about
how you can use

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:50.570
TensorFlow and ML to create
magical experiences in mobile's

00:02:50.570 --> 00:02:51.140
apps.

